# Executing Flows

## Overview

This Livebook runs one Flow four ways: to completion, with concurrency,
compiled without running, and under a managed Runner. It also shows a
failure. Read [Execution](execution.md) for the full contract.

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install The Package

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-beta.12"}])
```

## Define Actions And A Flow

```elixir
defmodule FlowExecution.Add do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_execution_add"

  @impl true
  def run(%{left: left, right: right}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{value: left + right}}
  end
end

defmodule FlowExecution.Combine do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_execution_combine"

  @impl true
  def run(%{left: left, right: right}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{total: left + right}}
  end
end

defmodule FlowExecution.Diamond do
  use Jido.Flow, name: "flow_execution_diamond"

  flow do
    step "left",
      action: FlowExecution.Add,
      params: %{left: input(:value), right: 1}

    step "right",
      action: FlowExecution.Add,
      params: %{left: input(:value), right: 2}

    step "combine",
      action: FlowExecution.Combine,
      params: %{
        left: result("left", :value),
        right: result("right", :value)
      }

    output(%{result: result("combine")})
  end
end
```

## Run To Completion

```elixir
{:ok, %{result: %{total: 13}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowExecution.Diamond, %{value: 5})
```

`"left"` and `"right"` do not depend on each other. With
`max_concurrency` above `1`, they can run at the same time. The result is the
same either way:

```elixir
{:ok, result} =
  Jido.Exec.run(
    FlowExecution.Diamond,
    %{value: 5},
    %{},
    max_concurrency: 2
  )
```

## Compile And Inspect

```elixir
{:ok, %Runic.Workflow{}} = Jido.Exec.compile(FlowExecution.Diamond)

{:ok, dependencies} = Jido.Flow.dependencies(FlowExecution.Diamond.flow())
dependencies["combine"]
```

`compile/2` checks every target and returns the executable workflow without
running it. Use it in tests and tools to catch target errors early.

## Observe Failure

```elixir
defmodule FlowExecution.Fail do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_execution_fail"

  @impl true
  def run(_params, _context), do: {:error, :not_available}
end

defmodule FlowExecution.Failing do
  use Jido.Flow, name: "flow_execution_failing"

  flow do
    step "fail", action: FlowExecution.Fail, params: %{}
    output(%{result: result("fail")})
  end
end
```

```elixir
{:error, error} = Jido.Exec.run(FlowExecution.Failing)
Exception.message(error)
```

The Action's error is returned unchanged, with the failing component in
`details.node`. Exec starts no new work after a failure. See
[Errors](errors.md).

## Managed Execution

A supervised application can run the same Flow under a `Runic.Runner`. The
Runner keeps running after the caller exits and can checkpoint and resume the
work. In an application, add the Runner to your supervision tree. Here, start
it directly:

```elixir
{:ok, _runner} = Runic.Runner.start_link(name: FlowExecution.Runner)

parent = self()

{:ok, _worker} =
  Jido.Exec.start(
    FlowExecution.Runner,
    "diamond-1",
    FlowExecution.Diamond,
    %{value: 5},
    %{},
    on_complete: fn execution_id, _workflow -> send(parent, {:done, execution_id}) end
  )
```

`start/6` returns before the work runs. Wait for `on_complete`, then read the
result fact:

```elixir
receive do
  {:done, "diamond-1"} -> :ok
after
  5_000 -> raise "the managed execution did not finish"
end

{:ok, workflow} = Runic.Runner.get_workflow(FlowExecution.Runner, "diamond-1")
Jido.Exec.result(workflow)
```

Use `Runic.Runner.checkpoint/2` and `stop/3` for durable lifecycle control.
Resume with `Jido.Exec.resume/4` and the same context and options. The Runic
Store contains progress. Flow JSON contains only the portable definition. See
[Managed Execution](managed-execution.md) for options, manual stepping,
checkpoints, and resume.
